zΔ⚡b|=1c12|+Algorithm %3D%3D%0AThe operation of {{Mono%7Cdiff}} is based on solving the [[longest common subsequence problem]].<ref name%3D"diff paper" />%0A%0AIn this problem, given two sequences of items:%0A%0A {{underline%7Ca}} {{underline%7Cb}} {{underline%7Cc}} {{underline%7Cd}} {{underline%7Cf}} {{underline%7Cg}} h {{underline%7Cj}} q {{underline%7Cz}}%0A%0A {{underline%7Ca}} {{underline%7Cb}} {{underline%7Cc}} {{underline%7Cd}} e {{underline%7Cf}} {{underline%7Cg}} i {{underline%7Cj}} k r x y {{underline%7Cz}}%0A%0Aand we want to find a longest sequence of items that is present in both original sequences in the same order. That is, we want to find a new sequence which can be obtained from the first original sequence by deleting some items, and from the second original sequence by deleting other items. We also want this sequence to be as long as possible. In this case it is%0A%0A a b c d  f  g  j  z%0A%0AFrom a longest common subsequence it is only a small step to get {{Mono%7Cdiff}}%2Dlike output: if an item is absent in the subsequence but present in the first original sequence, it must have been deleted (as indicated by the '%2D' marks, below). If it is absent in the subsequence but present in the second original sequence, it must have been inserted (as indicated by the '%2B' marks).%0A%0A e   h i   q   k r x y%0A %2B   %2D %2B   %2D   %2B %2B %2B %2B%0A%0A%3D%3D Usage %3D%3D%0AThe <code>diff</code> command is invoked from the command line, passing it the names of two files: <code>diff ''original'' ''new''</code>. The output of the command represents the changes required to transform the ''original'' file into the ''new'' file.%0A%0AIf ''original'' and ''new'' are directories, then {{Mono%7Cdiff}} will be run on each file that exists in both directories. An option, <code>%2Dr</code>, will recursively descend any matching subdirectories to compare files between directories.%0A%0AAny of the examples in the article use the following two files, ''original'' and ''new'':%0A{{Col%2Dbegin}}%0A{{Col%2Dbreak%7Cwidth%3D33%25}}%0A''original'':%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"text" line>%0AThis part of the%0Adocument has stayed the%0Asame from version to%0Aversion.  It shouldn't%0Abe shown if it doesn't%0Achange.  Otherwise, that%0Awould not be helping to%0Acompress the size of the%0Achanges.%0A%0AThis paragraph contains%0Atext that is outdated.%0AIt will be deleted in the%0Anear future.%0A%0AIt is important to spell%0Acheck this dokument. On%0Athe other hand, a%0Amisspelled word isn't%0Athe end of the world.%0ANothing in the rest of%0Athis paragraph needs to%0Abe changed. Things can%0Abe added after it.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A{{col%2Dbreak}}%0A''new'':%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"text" line>%0AThis is an important%0Anotice! It should%0Atherefore be located at%0Athe beginning of this%0Adocument!%0A%0AThis part of the%0Adocument has stayed the%0Asame from version to%0Aversion.  It shouldn't%0Abe shown if it doesn't%0Achange.  Otherwise, that%0Awould not be helping to%0Acompress the size of the%0Achanges.%0A%0AIt is important to spell%0Acheck this document. On%0Athe other hand, a%0Amisspelled word isn't%0Athe end of the world.%0ANothing in the rest of%0Athis paragraph needs to%0Abe changed. Things can%0Abe added after it.%0A%0AThis paragraph contains%0Aimportant new additions%0Ato this document.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A{{col%2Dbreak%7Cwidth%3D33%25}}%0AThe command '''<code>diff original new</code>''' produces the following ''normal diff output'':%0A{{pre%7C%0A0a1,6%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C> This is an important%0A> notice! It should%0A> therefore be located at%0A> the beginning of this%0A> document!%0A>}}%0A11,15d16%0A{{font color%7Cdarkred%7C< This paragraph contains%0A< text that is outdated.%0A< It will be deleted in the%0A< near future.%0A<}}%0A17c18%0A{{font color%7Cdarkred%7C< check this dokument. On}}%0A%2D%2D%2D%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C> check this document. On}}%0A24a26,29%0A{{font color%7Cdarkgreen%7C>%0A> This paragraph contains%0A> important new additions%0A> to this document.}}}}%0A%0A{{Note2}} ''Here, the diff output is shown with colors to make it easier to read. The diff utility does not produce colored output; its output is [[plain text]]. However, many tools can show the output with colors by using [[syntax highlighting]].''%0A{{col%2Dend}}%0AIn this traditional output format, '''<samp>a</samp>''' stands for ''added'', '''<samp>d</samp>''' for ''deleted'' and '''<samp>c</samp>''' for ''changed''. Line numbers of the original file appear before <samp>a</samp>/<samp>d</samp>/<samp>c</samp> and those of the new file appear after. The [[Less%2Dthan sign%7Cless%2Dthan]] and [[Greater%2Dthan sign%7Cgreater%2Dthan]] signs (at the beginning of lines that are added, deleted or changed) indicate which file the lines appear in. Addition lines are added to the original file to appear in the new file. Deletion lines are deleted from the original file to be missing in the new file.%0A%0ABy default, lines common to both files are not shown. Lines that have moved are shown as added at their new location and as deleted from their old location.<ref>{{cite book%7Ctitle%3DComparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/%7Cauthor1%3DDavid MacKenzie %7Cauthor2%3DPaul Eggert %7Cauthor3%3DRichard Stallman %7Cisbn%3D978%2D0%2D9541617%2D5%2D0%7Cpublisher%3DNetwork Theory%7Cyear%3D1997%7Clocation%3DBristol}}</ref> However, some diff tools highlight moved lines.%0A%0A%3D%3D Output {{vanchor%7Cvariations}} %3D%3D%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Edit script %3D%3D%3D%0AAn [[Ed (text editor)%7Ced script]] can still be generated by modern versions of diff with the <code>%2De</code> option. The resulting edit script for this example is as follows:%0A%0A 24'''a'''%0A %0A ''This paragraph contains''%0A ''important new additions''%0A ''to this document.''%0A .%0A 17'''c'''%0A ''check this document. On''%0A .%0A 11,15'''d'''%0A 0'''a'''%0A ''This is an important''%0A ''notice! It should''%0A ''therefore be located at''%0A ''the beginning of this''%0A ''document!''%0A %0A .%0A%0AIn order to transform the content of file ''original'' into the content of file ''new'' using {{Mono%7Ced}}, we should append two lines to this diff file, one line containing a <code>w</code> (write) command, and one containing a <code>q</code> (quit) command (e.g. by {{code%7Clang%3Dbash%7Cprintf "w\nq\n" >> mydiff}}). Here we gave the diff file the name ''mydiff'' and the transformation will then happen when we run {{code%7Clang%3Dbash%7Ced %2Ds original < mydiff}}.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Context format %3D%3D%3D%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the ''context format'' ({{code%7C%2Dc}}) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures ({{code%7C%2Dr}}), adding those features in 2.8 BSD, released in July 1981. The context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0AIn the context format, any changed lines are shown alongside unchanged lines before and after. The inclusion of any number of unchanged lines provides a ''context'' to the patch. The ''context'' consists of lines that have not changed between the two files and serve as a reference to locate the lines' place in a modified file and find the intended location for a change to be applied regardless of whether the line numbers still correspond. The context format introduces greater readability for humans and reliability when applying the patch, and an output which is accepted as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. This intelligent behavior isn't possible with the traditional diff output.%0A%0AThe number of unchanged lines shown above and below a change ''hunk'' can be defined by the user, even zero, but three lines is typically the default. If the context of unchanged lines in a hunk overlap with an adjacent hunk, then diff will avoid duplicating the unchanged lines and merge the hunks into a single hunk.%0A%0AA "{{code%7C!}}" represents a change between lines that correspond in the two files. A "{{code%7C%2B}}" represents the addition of a line, while a blank [[space (punctuation)%7Cspace]] represents an unchanged line. At the beginning of the patch is the file information, including the full path and a [[time stamp]] delimited by a tab character. At the beginning of each hunk are the line numbers that apply for the corresponding change in the files. A number range appearing between sets of three asterisks applies to the original file, while sets of three dashes apply to the new file. The hunk ranges specify the starting and ending line numbers in the respective file.%0A%0AThe command {{code%7Cdiff %2Dc original new}} produces the following output:%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"diff">%0A*** /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A***************%0A*** 1,3 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 1,9 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A%2B This is an important%0A%2B notice! It should%0A%2B therefore be located at%0A%2B the beginning of this%0A%2B document!%0A%2B%0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A***************%0A*** 8,20 ****%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A%2D This paragraph contains%0A%2D text that is outdated.%0A%2D It will be deleted in the%0A%2D near future.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this dokument. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A%2D%2D%2D 14,21 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this document. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A***************%0A*** 22,24 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 23,29 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2B This paragraph contains%0A%2B important new additions%0A%2B to this document.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A{{Note2}} ''Here, the diff output is shown with colors to make it easier to read. The diff utility does not produce colored output; its output is [[plain text]]. However, many tools can show the output with colors by using [[syntax highlighting]].''%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Unified format %3D%3D%3D%0AThe ''unified format'' (or ''unidiff'')<ref>{{Cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Detailed%2DUnified.html%7Ctitle%3DDetailed Description of Unified Format%7Cwebsite%3DGNU Diffutils (version 3.7, 7 January 2018)}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread%3D164293%7Ctitle%3DUnified Diff Format%7Clast%3Dvan Rossum%7Cfirst%3DGuido%7Cwebsite%3DAll Things Pythonic}}</ref> inherits the technical improvements made by the context format, but produces a smaller diff with old and new text presented immediately adjacent. Unified format is usually invoked using the "<code>%2Du</code>" [[command line option]]. This output is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. Many projects specifically request that "diffs" be submitted in the unified format, making unified diff format the most common format for exchange between software developers.%0A%0AUnified context diffs were originally developed by Wayne Davison in August 1990 (in '''unidiff''' which appeared in Volume 14 of comp.sources.misc). [[Richard Stallman]] added unified diff support to the [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff utility one month later, and the feature debuted in '''GNU diff''' 1.15, released in January 1991. GNU diff has since generalized the context format to allow arbitrary formatting of diffs.%0A%0AThe format starts with the same two%2Dline [[Header (computing)%7Cheader]] as the context format, except that the original file is preceded by "<samp>%2D%2D%2D</samp>" and the new file is preceded by "<samp>%2B%2B%2B</samp>". Following this are one or more '''change hunks''' that contain the line differences in the file. The unchanged, contextual lines are preceded by a space character, addition lines are preceded by a [[plus sign]], and deletion lines are preceded by a [[minus sign]].%0A%0AA hunk begins with '''range information''' and is immediately followed with the line additions, line deletions, and any number of the contextual lines. The range information is surrounded by double [[at sign]]s, and combines onto a single line what appears on two lines in the context format ([[#Context format%7Cabove]]). The format of the range information line is as follows:%0A%0A @@ %2Dl,s %2Bl,s @@ ''optional section heading''<!%2D%2D <syntaxhighlight lang%3D"text"> ruins the italics %2D%2D>%0A%0AThe hunk range information contains two hunk ranges. The range for the hunk of the original file is preceded by a minus symbol, and the range for the new file is preceded by a plus symbol. Each hunk range is of the format ''l,s'' where ''l'' is the starting line number and ''s'' is the number of lines the change hunk applies to for each respective file. In many versions of GNU diff, each range can omit the comma and trailing value ''s'', in which case ''s'' defaults to 1. Note that the only really interesting value is the ''l'' line number of the first range; all the other values can be computed from the diff.%0A%0AThe hunk range for the original should be the sum of all contextual and deletion (including changed) hunk lines. The hunk range for the new file should be a sum of all contextual and addition (including changed) hunk lines. If hunk size information does not correspond with the number of lines in the hunk, then the diff could be considered invalid and be rejected.%0A%0AOptionally, the hunk range can be followed by the heading of the section or function that the hunk is part of. This is mainly useful to make the diff easier to read. When creating a diff with GNU diff, the heading is identified by [[regular expression]] matching.<ref>[https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Sections.html 2.2.3 Showing Which Sections Differences Are in], GNU diffutils manual</ref>%0A%0AIf a line is modified, it is represented as a deletion and addition. Since the hunks of the original and new file appear in the same hunk, such changes would appear adjacent to one another.<ref>[http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread%3D164293 Unified Diff Format] by [[Guido van Rossum]], June 14, 2006</ref>%0AAn occurrence of this in the example below is:%0A%0A<pre>%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A</pre>%0A%0AThe command <code>diff %2Du original new</code> produces the following output:%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"diff">%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2B%2B%2B /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A@@ %2D1,3 %2B1,9 @@%0A%2BThis is an important%0A%2Bnotice! It should%0A%2Btherefore be located at%0A%2Bthe beginning of this%0A%2Bdocument!%0A%2B%0A This part of the%0A document has stayed the%0A same from version to%0A@@ %2D8,13 %2B14,8 @@%0A compress the size of the%0A changes.%0A%0A%2DThis paragraph contains%0A%2Dtext that is outdated.%0A%2DIt will be deleted in the%0A%2Dnear future.%0A%2D%0A It is important to spell%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A the other hand, a%0A misspelled word isn't%0A the end of the world.%0A@@ %2D22,3 %2B23,7 @@%0A this paragraph needs to%0A be changed. Things can%0A be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2BThis paragraph contains%0A%2Bimportant new additions%0A%2Bto this document.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A{{Note2}} ''Here, the diff output is shown with colors to make it easier to read. The diff utility does not produce colored output; its output is [[plain text]]. However, many tools can show the output with colors by using [[syntax highlighting]].''%0A%0ANote that to successfully separate the file names from the timestamps, the delimiter between them is a tab character. This is invisible on screen and can be lost when diffs are copy/pasted from console/terminal screens.%0A%0AThere are some modifications and extensions to the diff formats that are used and understood by certain programs and in certain contexts. For example, some [[revision control]] systems—such as [[Subversion (software)%7CSubversion]]—specify a version number, "working copy", or any other comment instead of or in addition to a timestamp in the diff's header section.%0A%0ASome tools allow diffs for several different files to be merged into one, using a header for each modified file that may look something like this:%0A%0A Index: path/to/file.cpp%0A%0AThe special case of files that do not end in a newline is not handled. Neither the unidiff utility nor the POSIX diff standard define a way to handle this type of files. (Indeed, such files are not "text" files by strict POSIX definitions.<ref>http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_205 Section 3.206</ref>)%0AThe [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program is not aware even of an implementation specific diff output.%0A%0A%3D%3D |=2945|+%0A%0A%3D%3DExternal links%3D%3D%0A{{Wikibooks%7CGuide to Unix%7CCommands}}%0A{{Prone to spam%7Cdate%3DMay 2012}}%0A{{Z148}}<!%2D%2D     {{No more links}}%0A%0A       Please be cautious adding more external links.%0A%0AWikipedia is not a collection of links and should not be used for advertising.%0A%0A     Excessive or inappropriate links will be removed.%0A%0A See [[Wikipedia:External links]] & [[Wikipedia:Spam]] for details.%0A%0AIf there are already suitable links, propose additions or replacements on%0Athe article's talk page, or submit your link to the relevant category at %0Athe Open Directory Project (dmoz.org) and link there using {{Dmoz}}.%0A%0A%2D%2D>%0A*{{man%7Ccu%7Cdiff%7CSUS%7Ccompare two files}}%0A* {{man%7C1%7Cdiff%7CPlan 9}}%0A* {{man%7C1%7Cdiff%7CInferno}}%0A*{{Dmoz%7CComputers/Software/File_Management/File_Comparison%7CFile comparison}}%0A*[http://www.bodurov.com/JavaScriptDiff/ JavaScript Implementation]%0A%0A{{Unix commands}}%0A{{Version control software}}%0A%0A[[Category:1974 software]]%0A[[Category:Free file comparison tools]]%0A[[Category:Formal languages]]%0A[[Category:Pattern matching]]%0A[[Category:Data differencing]]%0A[[Category:Standard Unix programs]]%0A[[Category:Unix SUS2008 utilities]]%0A[[Category:Plan 9 commands]]%0A[[Category:Inferno (operating system) commands]]|
