zΔ⚡b|+{{About%7Cthe utility program%7Cthe general topic of file comparison%7CFile comparison%7Cdiffs in Wikipedia%7C:help:diff%7Cother uses%7CDIFF (disambiguation){{!}}DIFF}}|=1|+{{lowercase%7Ctitle%3Ddiff}}|=2fa|+[[computing]], the utility '''diff''' is a [[data comparison]] tool that computes and displays the differences between the contents of files. Unlike [[edit distance]] notions used for other purposes, diff is line%2Doriented rather than character%2Doriented, but it is like [[Levenshtein distance]] in that it tries to determine the smallest set of deletions and insertions to create one file from the other. The utility displays the changes in one of several standard formats, such that both humans or computers can parse the changes, and use them for [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatching]].%0A%0ATypically, ''diff'' is used to show the changes between two versions of the same file. Modern implementations also support [[binary file]]s.<ref>MacKenzie ''et al.''  "Binary Files and Forcing Text Comparison" in ''Comparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch''.  Downloaded 28 April 2007.  [https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Binary.html]</ref> The output is called a "diff", or a [[patch (computing)%7Cpatch]], since the output can be applied with the [[Unix]] program [[patch (Unix)%7C{{Mono%7Cpatch}}]]. The output of similar file comparison utilities are also called a "diff"; like the use of the word "[[grep]]" for describing the act of searching, the word ''diff'' became a generic term for calculating data difference and the results thereof.<ref>Eric S. Raymond (ed.), [http://catb.org/jargon/html/D/diff.html "diff"], ''The Jargon File'', version 4.4.7</ref> The [[POSIX]] standard specifies |=48|+<ref>{{cite book%7Cauthor1 %3D IEEE Computer Society%7Cauthor2%2Dlink %3D The Open Group%7Cauthor2 %3D The Open Group%7Cdate%3D26 September 2008%7Ctitle %3D Standard for Information Technology&mdash;Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX) Base Specifications, Issue 7%7Cpages %3D 2599–2607%7Cauthor1%2Dlink %3D IEEE Computer Society}} IEEE Std. 1003.1%2D2001 specifies traditional, "ed script", and context diff output formats; IEEE Std. 1003.1%2D2008 added the (by then more common) unified format.</ref>|=4faa|+<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DE. Myers%7Ctitle%3DAn O(ND) Difference Algorithm and Its Variations%7Cjournal%3DAlgorithmica%7Cvolume%3D1%7Cissue%3D2%7Cyear%3D1986%7Cpages%3D251–266%7Cdoi%3D10.1007/BF01840446%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.4.6927%7Cs2cid%3D6996809}}</ref>|=3f|+<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor1%3DWebb Miller %7Cauthor2%3DEugene W. Myers %7Ctitle%3DA File Comparison Program%7Cjournal%3DSoftware — Practice and Experience%7Cvolume%3D15%7Cissue%3D11%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D1025–1040%7Cdoi%3D10.1002/spe.4380151102%7Cciteseerx%3D10.1.1.189.70 }}</ref>|=70|+[[|=c|+]]|=1|+<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DEsko Ukkonen%7Ctitle%3DAlgorithms for Approximate String Matching%7Cvolume%3D64%7Cjournal%3DInformation and Control%7Cissue%3D1–3%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D100–118 %7C doi %3D 10.1016/S0019%2D9958(85)80046%2D2%7Cdoi%2Daccess%3Dfree}}</ref>|=67|+[[|=7|+]]|=dc|+[[Patch (Unix)%7C|=5|+]]|=13|+<ref>[https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/ GNU Diff utilities]. Made available by the [[Free Software Foundation]]. Free Documentation. Free source code.%0A</ref>|=81|+<ref>{{cite web %7Clast1%3DWaugh %7Cfirst1%3DTim %7Ctitle%3Dtwaugh/patchutils %7Curl%3Dhttps://github.com/twaugh/patchutils %7Cdate%3D12 June 2020}}</ref>|=ec|+{{citation needed%7Cdate%3DFebruary 2015}}{{discuss%7Ctopicality of front%2Dends}}|=2|+[[|=5|+]]|=108|+[[merge (revision control)%7C|=7|+]]|=a4|+[[|=5|+]]|=36e|+[[|=3|+]]|=1b9|+[[|=5|+]]|=870|+[[|=a|+]]|=1a5|+[[Space (punctuation)%7Cwhitespace]]|=183|+[[|=c|+]]|=1204|
